<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">18 February in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 18 February, from 1229 to 1943.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1229</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Frederick II regains Jerusalem by treaty</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In the Sixth Crusade, Emperor Frederick II agreed a ten-year truce with the Ayyubid Sultan al-Kamil that returned Jerusalem to Christian control without a battle. The deal angered many on both sides.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1564</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Michelangelo dies in Rome</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The sculptor, painter and architect died aged 88, still working as chief architect of St Peter's. His works include the David, the Sistine Chapel ceiling and the Last Judgement.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1678</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">John Bunyan's 'The Pilgrim's Progress' is published</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Partly written in Bedford gaol, Bunyan's allegory of Christian's journey to the Celestial City was an immediate success. It has been translated into more than 200 languages.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1885</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Mark Twain's 'Adventures of Huckleberry Finn' published in the US</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Twain's novel of a boy and an escaped slave on the Mississippi was published in America. Written in vernacular speech, it has been called the source of all modern American literature.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1930</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Clyde Tombaugh discovers Pluto</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Comparing photographic plates at Lowell Observatory, Tombaugh found a faint moving object. Named Pluto at the suggestion of 11-year-old Venetia Burney, it was reclassified as a dwarf planet in 2006.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1943</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Hans and Sophie Scholl arrested in Munich</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The siblings of the White Rose resistance group were caught distributing anti-Nazi leaflets at Munich University. They were tried and guillotined four days later.</p></li></ol>
